text:
Check your motherboard package for the following items.
<PIC:Manual25_1>
{{image description: A package contents table for an ASUS Gaming Motherboard Z170 PRO GAMING. Rows list the motherboard, 4 x Serial ATA 6.0 Gb/s cables, accessories such as I/O shield, ROG SLI bridge connector, ROG cable labels, M.2 screw package, CPU installation tool, plus support DVD and user guide.}}

Apply the Thermal Interface Material  to the CPU heatsink and CPU  before you install the heatsink and  fan if necessary.<PIC:Manual25_13>
{{image description: A close-up installation drawing showing thermal compound being applied from a syringe onto the center of a CPU in the socket.}},<PIC:Manual25_14>
{{image description: A standalone triangular caution symbol with an exclamation mark in the center on a mostly blank page area.}}


##### To install the CPU heatsink and fan assembly

<PIC:Manual25_15>
{{image description: CPU heatsink and fan installation diagram. Numbered arrows show placing the fan assembly on the CPU, pressing down the four push pins, and connecting the fan cable to the CPU_FAN header.}}

<PIC:Manual25_16>
{{image description: Continuation of heatsink installation. A drawing shows locking the push pins on the motherboard, with a small inset marking correct and incorrect pin positions using check and X symbols.}}

##### To uninstall the CPU heatsink and fan assembly

<PIC:Manual25_17>
{{image description: CPU fan removal diagram. Arrows show turning the push pins, pulling them upward, and lifting the heatsink and fan assembly away from the motherboard.}}

DDR4 3333 (O.C.) MHz capability<PIC:Manual25_21>
{{image description: A DDR4 memory Qualified Vendors List table with several 16GB kit rows from G.SKILL, Corsair, and A-DATA. Columns include part number, size, SS/DS, chip brand, chip number, timing, voltage, and 2-DIMM or 4-DIMM support.}},<PIC:Manual25_22>
{{image description: A short memory QVL table showing entries from G.SKILL and Corsair with part numbers, 16GB kit sizes, chip brand, timing, voltage, and DIMM socket support dots.}}

4.  LAN (RJ-45) port.  This port allows Gigabit connection to a Local Area Network (LAN)  through a network hub.
<PIC:Manual25_35>
{{image description: Rear panel connectors diagram with numbered ports 1 through 16. It shows PS/2, USB ports, VGA, DVI, HDMI, LAN, optical S/PDIF, audio jacks, and other rear I/O connectors.}}

LAN port LED indications
<PIC:Manual25_36>
{{image description: LAN port LED diagram showing Activity/Link LED and Speed LED positions above the RJ-45 port.}}

9.  Microphone port (pink). This port connects to a microphone.
<PIC:Manual25_37>
{{image description: LAN port LED indications table. Activity/Link LED status rows include Off for no link, Orange for linked, Orange blinking for data activity, and Orange blinking then steady for wake from S5 mode. Speed LED rows show Off for 10 Mbps, Orange for 100 Mbps, and Green for 1 Gbps.}}

Note:
<PIC:Manual25_47>
{{image description: Small note-style icon showing a pen or feather on an otherwise blank white page; no readable technical text is visible.}}

CAUTION:
<PIC:Manual25_53>
{{image description: Small warning icon on a mostly blank page, shown as a triangular sign with a lightning bolt inside. No other readable text is present.}}

Model name LED Lighting
<PIC:Manual25_56>
{{image description: Table describing model name LED lit modes. Breathing mode: The LEDs blink intermittently. Still mode: The LEDs become solid red.}}

To access the EZ Mode, click  EzMode (F7)  or press  <F7>.

<PIC:Manual25_67>
{{image description: ASUS UEFI BIOS Utility Advanced Mode screen with red callouts. It labels the menu bar, MyFavorite, Q-Fan control, EZ Tuning Wizard, Quick Note, Hot Keys, menu items, sub-menu item, general help, configuration fields, scroll bar, popup window, last modified settings, EZ Mode shortcut, and a hardware monitor sidebar.}}

The menu bar on top of the screen has the following main items:
<PIC:Manual25_68>
{{image description: Green table explaining BIOS menu bar tabs. My Favorites saves frequently used settings and configuration; Main changes basic system configuration; Ai Tweaker changes overclocking settings; Advanced changes advanced system settings; Monitor displays system temperature, power status, and fan settings; Boot changes system boot configuration; Tool configures special functions; Exit selects exit options and loads default settings.}}

3.  Select an item from main menu panel, then click the submenu that you want to save as  favorite from the submenu panel and click.
<PIC:Manual25_77>
{{image description: Instructional BIOS image showing steps to add favorites. Text says press F3 or click (F3)MyFavorite to open the Setup Tree Map screen, then select BIOS items to save. The screenshot labels Main menu panel, Submenu panel, and Selected items shortcut.}}

3.  Select a method of creating a modified Windows 7 installation file:
- Windows 7 OS disk to USB storage device  - Select  Windows 7 OS disk to USB storage device  then click  Next.<PIC:Manual25_90>
{{image description: ASUS EZ Installer window. It explains creating a copy of a Windows 7 installation file into a USB storage device or optical disc. A dropdown says Windows 7 OS disc to USB storage device, a note warns that an 8 GB or larger USB device will be formatted, and a Next button is visible.}}

- Select  Windows 7 OS disk to ISO file  then click  Next.
- Check  I agree  and then click  Next.<PIC:Manual25_95>
{{image description: ASUS EZ Installer EULA screen for the ISO-file workflow, with license text, an I agree checkbox, Back button, and Next button.}}

